  "gold_solution": "```python\nfrom gurobipy import *\n\ndef solve_problem():\n    m = Model(\"JSSP\")\n    m.setParam('TimeLimit', 300)\n\n    # --- Data ---\n    n_jobs = 6\n    n_machines = 3\n    processing_times = [[13, 18, 19], [19, 8, 24], [24, 12, 25], [11, 19, 26], [29, 11, 7], [8, 11, 6]]\n    machine_assignments = [[2, 1, 0], [1, 0, 2], [2, 0, 1], [1, 2, 0], [0, 2, 1], [1, 0, 2]]\n\n    # Big-M: sum of all processing times\n    M = sum(processing_times[j][o] for j in range(n_jobs) for o in range(n_machines))\n\n    # --- Variables ---\n    # Start times for each operation\n    S = {}\n    for j in range(n_jobs):\n        for o in range(n_machines):\n            S[(j,o)] = m.addVar(lb=0, ub=M, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=f\"Start_{j}_{o}\")\n\n    # Makespan variable\n    Cmax = m.addVar(lb=0, ub=M, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=\"Cmax\")\n\n    # Build machine -> [(job, op)] mapping\n    machine_ops = {mach: [] for mach in range(n_machines)}\n    for j in range(n_jobs):\n        for o in range(n_machines):\n            machine_ops[machine_assignments[j][o]].append((j, o))\n\n    # Disjunctive binary variables for each pair on same machine\n    Y = {}\n    for mach in range(n_machines):\n        ops = machine_ops[mach]\n        for i in range(len(ops)):\n            for k in range(i+1, len(ops)):\n                j1, o1 = ops[i]\n                j2, o2 = ops[k]\n                Y[(j1,o1,j2,o2)] = m.addVar(vtype=GRB.BINARY, name=f\"Y_{j1}_{o1}_{j2}_{o2}\")\n\n    # --- Objective: minimize makespan ---\n    m.setObjective(Cmax, GRB.MINIMIZE)\n\n    # --- Constraints ---\n    # 1. Precedence: operations within a job must follow order\n    for j in range(n_jobs):\n        for o in range(n_machines - 1):\n            m.addConstr(\n                S[(j,o+1)] >= S[(j,o)] + processing_times[j][o],\n                name=f\"Prec_{j}_{o}\")\n\n    # 2. Disjunctive: no two operations on the same machine overlap\n    for mach in range(n_machines):\n        ops = machine_ops[mach]\n        for i in range(len(ops)):\n            for k in range(i+1, len(ops)):\n                j1, o1 = ops[i]\n                j2, o2 = ops[k]\n                p1 = processing_times[j1][o1]\n                p2 = processing_times[j2][o2]\n                y = Y[(j1,o1,j2,o2)]\n                m.addConstr(\n                    S[(j1,o1)] + p1 <= S[(j2,o2)] + M * (1 - y),\n                    name=f\"Disj_A_{j1}_{o1}_{j2}_{o2}\")\n                m.addConstr(\n                    S[(j2,o2)] + p2 <= S[(j1,o1)] + M * y,\n                    name=f\"Disj_B_{j1}_{o1}_{j2}_{o2}\")\n\n    # 3. Makespan: Cmax >= completion of last operation of each job\n    for j in range(n_jobs):\n        last_op = n_machines - 1\n        m.addConstr(\n            Cmax >= S[(j, last_op)] + processing_times[j][last_op],\n            name=f\"Makespan_J{j}\")\n\n    m.optimize()\n    return m\n```",
  "LLM_description": "Subject: Coordinated Production Scheduling Across Specialized Workstations\n\nTo: Plant Leadership and Production Planning Team\nFrom: Manufacturing Operations Manager\nDate: [Today\u2019s Date]\n\nContext\nOur facility operates multiple specialized machines (workstations) that perform distinct manufacturing steps. We are preparing to schedule a set of customer orders (jobs) that each require a defined series of operations. The processing route for each job is fixed and may differ from other jobs, reflecting the unique capabilities of each workstation and the product\u2019s technical requirements.\n\nScheduling Challenge\nWe must determine when each operation should start on its designated machine so that all jobs flow smoothly through the shop. Processing times vary across operations\u2014from relatively short tasks to longer, more resource-intensive steps\u2014so poor sequencing can create bottlenecks on some machines while others sit idle. The volume is meaningful but manageable, with multiple operations per job and limited machine capacity, making the timing and hand-offs between machines critical to overall throughput.\n\nOperating Requirements\n- Fixed job sequences: Operations within the same job must be completed in order; no job can proceed to its next step until the prior step is finished.\n- Machine specificity: Each operation is assigned to a particular machine and must be performed there for its known duration.\n- Machine exclusivity: Each machine can process only one operation at a time; overlapping work on the same machine is not allowed.\n- No preemption: Once an operation begins on a machine, it runs to completion without interruption or transfer.\n\nGoal\nBuild a feasible, conflict-free production schedule that sequences all operations on their respective machines and completes all jobs in the shortest possible total time (overall completion time, often called makespan), while adhering to the rules above. A well-constructed schedule will minimize waiting between steps, keep bottleneck machines effectively utilized, and deliver all orders promptly.\n\nAnnex \u2013 Production Data\n- Number of jobs: 6\n- Number of machines (workstations): 3\n- Job-by-job production schedule (operations must be performed in the listed sequence):\n  Job 5: Op1 on Machine 0 for 29 time units \u2192 Op2 on Machine 2 for 11 time units \u2192 Op3 on Machine 1 for 7 time units\n  Job 3: Op1 on Machine 2 for 24 time units \u2192 Op2 on Machine 0 for 12 time units \u2192 Op3 on Machine 1 for 25 time units\n  Job 2: Op1 on Machine 1 for 19 time units \u2192 Op2 on Machine 0 for 8 time units \u2192 Op3 on Machine 2 for 24 time units\n  Job 6: Op1 on Machine 1 for 8 time units \u2192 Op2 on Machine 0 for 11 time units \u2192 Op3 on Machine 2 for 6 time units\n  Job 1: Op1 on Machine 2 for 13 time units \u2192 Op2 on Machine 1 for 18 time units \u2192 Op3 on Machine 0 for 19 time units\n  Job 4: Op1 on Machine 1 for 11 time units \u2192 Op2 on Machine 2 for 19 time units \u2192 Op3 on Machine 0 for 26 time units",
  "LLM_description_template": "Subject: Coordinated Production Scheduling Across Specialized Workstations\n\nTo: Plant Leadership and Production Planning Team\nFrom: Manufacturing Operations Manager\nDate: [Today\u2019s Date]\n\nContext\nOur facility operates multiple specialized machines (workstations) that perform distinct manufacturing steps. We are preparing to schedule a set of customer orders (jobs) that each require a defined series of operations. The processing route for each job is fixed and may differ from other jobs, reflecting the unique capabilities of each workstation and the product\u2019s technical requirements.\n\nScheduling Challenge\nWe must determine when each operation should start on its designated machine so that all jobs flow smoothly through the shop. Processing times vary across operations\u2014from relatively short tasks to longer, more resource-intensive steps\u2014so poor sequencing can create bottlenecks on some machines while others sit idle. The volume is meaningful but manageable, with multiple operations per job and limited machine capacity, making the timing and hand-offs between machines critical to overall throughput.\n\nOperating Requirements\n- Fixed job sequences: Operations within the same job must be completed in order; no job can proceed to its next step until the prior step is finished.\n- Machine specificity: Each operation is assigned to a particular machine and must be performed there for its known duration.\n- Machine exclusivity: Each machine can process only one operation at a time; overlapping work on the same machine is not allowed.\n- No preemption: Once an operation begins on a machine, it runs to completion without interruption or transfer.\n\nGoal\nBuild a feasible, conflict-free production schedule that sequences all operations on their respective machines and completes all jobs in the shortest possible total time (overall completion time, often called makespan), while adhering to the rules above. A well-constructed schedule will minimize waiting between steps, keep bottleneck machines effectively utilized, and deliver all orders promptly.\n\nANNEX \u2013 Production Data\n- Number of jobs: {NUM_JOBS}\n- Number of machines (workstations): {NUM_MACHINES}\n- Machine assignments by job and operation: {MACHINE_ASSIGNMENTS}\n- Processing times by job and operation (time units): {PROCESSING_TIMES}"
